\begin_layout Subsection
t-SNE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Again,
 I use the implementation in sklearn to fit t-SNE.
 I use a random initialization with a fixed seed and gradient descent to find a local minimum of the non-convex KL-divergence loss.
 Figure 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
ref{fig:dataviz-2-1-1-1}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 presents the results for both raw and standardized data,
 using perplexities of 30,
 100,
 and 300.
 
\begin_inset Float figure
placement document
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename output/tSNE.png
	scale 40

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
t-SNE results for different choices of perplexity
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:dataviz-2-1-1-1"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the raw data,
 t-SNE reveals several distinct circular structures.
 These correspond to different loop-shaped regions of the two intertwined point clouds in the original three-dimensional data,
 rather than necessarily representing separate clusters.
 At perplexity 30,
 some loops are distorted or broken,
 while perplexity 300 compresses the structures and brings them closer together.
 I would therefore choose perplexity 100,
 which produces the clearest separation while preserving the approximately circular shapes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the standardized data,
 the structures become less clearly separated,
 particularly at perplexity 300,
 where several loops overlap.
 This illustrates how standardization changes the neighborhood relationships that t-SNE attempts to preserve.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Compared with PCA,
 t-SNE better reveals the local nonlinear structure of the data.
 However,
 the relative positions and distances between the resulting loops cannot be interpreted as accurately reflecting their geometry in the original three-dimensional space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Determining the Number of Clusters
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relevant non-trivial choices of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in\{2,3,\dots,21\}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1$
\end_inset

 assigns all points to one cluster (thus 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}$
\end_inset

 will be the centroid) and 
\begin_inset Formula $k=22$
\end_inset

 assigns one 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 to each point 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}=x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To determine the appropriate number of clusters i use the heuristic method of finding the 
\emph on
elbow point
\emph default
.
 This is the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 whereafter the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-means cost stops decreasing 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

rapidly
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Therefore we strike a kind of balance between parameters and loss.
 
\end_layout

